    <string name="settings_about_app">About CoverIt</string>
    <string name="about_subtitle">For when you have to show a paper, but not all of it.</string>
    <string name="about_story">This app was born from a simple need: sending a document without showing more than necessary. A medical report, a contract, a payslip or a photo of an ID card carry data nobody else needs to see. The tools out there upload your files to a server, ask for an account, add watermarks, or just draw a black box that can be removed later. So I built CoverIt: free, no ads, no accounts and no internet connection. Everything happens on your phone, and what you cover is really erased from the file.</string>
    <string name="about_coffee">Buy me a coffee</string>
    <string name="about_more_apps">See my other apps</string>
    <string name="made_by">Made with ❤️ by %1$s</string>
    <string name="dev_handle">@InlitX</string>
    <string name="about_legal">© InlitX. CoverIt is offered as is, with no warranty. Always check a document before sending it.</string>
    <string name="letter_title">About your privacy</string>
    <string name="letter_sub">A letter from the person who made CoverIt</string>
    <string name="letter_greeting">Hi,</string>
    <string name="letter_1">Documents say a lot about us: our ID number, where we live, what we earn, what illness we have, who our family is. Once a file leaves your phone you no longer decide where it ends up, who copies it or how long it is kept. A photo of an ID card sent to the wrong place is enough to impersonate someone. That is why privacy matters: it is not about hiding anything, it is about deciding who sees what.</string>
    <string name="letter_2">CoverIt never connects to the internet. It does not even ask Android for permission to do so, so it could not send anything even if it wanted to. There are no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no trackers. The documents you open, what the app detects and the rules you teach it stay on this phone.</string>
    <string name="letter_3">Covering is not drawing a box on top. In a PDF, every page with something covered is turned into an image with the cover already painted in, and only the words you kept are written back as text, so the covered text no longer exists anywhere in the file. Pages with nothing covered are copied as they were. The new PDF is built from scratch, without author, hidden metadata, forms, scripts, annotations or earlier versions.</string>
    <string name="letter_4">In Word, Excel, PowerPoint and LibreOffice files, every covered letter is replaced by a block inside the file itself, so it still opens and can be edited. The same text is also blanked wherever else it appears: links, alternative text, sheet names. Comments and their authors, tracked changes (deleted text is often still inside), the author and company, and the preview image are removed, and pictures inside lose their location data. Photos are saved again from scratch, without EXIF, GPS or camera details.</string>
    <string name="letter_5">After covering, CoverIt reads the copy again from scratch and checks that nothing you covered can still be found in it and that no metadata is left. That is the check mark you see at the end.</string>
    <string name="letter_6">Your documents are kept in the app\'s private space, which other apps cannot read, and Android does not back them up to the cloud. You decide whether to keep the original next to the clean copy, and you can delete everything at once from Settings. When you share a copy, it goes only to the app you choose; from then on it is in their hands.</string>
    <string name="letter_7">I also want to be honest about the limits. Automatic detection helps, but it can miss things or mark things that are not personal, so look at every page before sending. Text inside photos and scanned pages is not read yet, so cover it by hand. Black is the safest style; blur and pixelation are also painted onto the image, but they show the size and shape of what is underneath.</string>
    <string name="letter_8">CoverIt\'s code is open, so anyone can check that this letter tells the truth.</string>
    <string name="letter_closing">Thank you for trusting it with your papers.</string>
